# Chapter 5: At the health center.

<#ch-health>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    dcterms:title "At the health center"@en ,
                  "På vårdcentralen"@sv ,
                  "Im Gesundheitszentrum"@de ,
                  "En el centro de salud"@es ,
                  "Au centre de santé"@fr ,
                  "보건소에서"@ko ;
    dcterms:description "Sam falls ill: calling the health center, telling the doctor how long it has been going on, and getting something for the throat at the pharmacy. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ,
                        "Sam blir sjuk: ringa vårdcentralen, berätta för läkaren hur länge det har hållit på och köpa något mot halsen på apoteket. Avslutas med en repetition av kapitlet."@sv ,
                        "Sam wird krank: im Gesundheitszentrum anrufen, dem Arzt erzählen, wie lange es schon andauert, und in der Apotheke etwas für den Hals holen. Endet mit einer Wiederholung des Kapitels."@de ,
                        "Sam enferma: llamar al centro de salud, decirle al médico cuánto tiempo lleva así y conseguir algo para la garganta en la farmacia. Termina con un repaso del capítulo."@es ,
                        "Sam tombe malade : appeler le centre de santé, dire au médecin depuis combien de temps ça dure et prendre quelque chose pour la gorge à la pharmacie. Se termine par une révision du chapitre."@fr ,
                        "샘이 병이 납니다. 보건소에 전화하기, 언제부터 아팠는지 의사에게 말하기, 약국에서 목에 좋은 약 사기를 다룹니다. 챕터 복습으로 끝납니다."@ko ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-health-r01> ,
                              <#q-health-r02> .

<#ch-health-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-health> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Calling the health center

*A Monday morning in November. Sam has felt ill since Saturday and still has a sore throat and a fever, and calls the health center nearby.*

**Sjuksköterskan:** Vårdcentralen, hej. Vad gäller det?\\
*Health center, hello. What is it about?*

**Sam:** Hej, jag har ont i halsen och feber.\\
*Hi, I have a sore throat and a fever.*

**Sjuksköterskan:** Hur hög feber har du?\\
*How high is your fever?*

**Sam:** Trettioåtta och fem i morse.\\
*Thirty-eight point five this morning.*

**Sjuksköterskan:** Har du svårt att andas?\\
*Do you have trouble breathing?*

**Sam:** Nej, men det gör ont när jag sväljer.\\
*No, but it hurts when I swallow.*

**Sjuksköterskan:** Du kan få en tid hos en läkare i eftermiddag, klockan två. Passar det?\\
*You can have an appointment with a doctor this afternoon, at two. Does that suit you?*

**Sam:** Ja, det passar bra.\\
*Yes, that suits me fine.*

**Sjuksköterskan:** Om du blir sämre innan dess, kan du ringa 1177. Är det akut, ring 112.\\
*If you get worse before then, you can call 1177. If it's an emergency, call 112.*

---

**Vårdcentralen**: the local health center, where you go first with most illnesses. You call it, or contact it online, and a nurse (*sjuksköterska*) often answers first and decides whether you need to see a doctor (*läkare*).

**Vad gäller det?**: "What is it about?", how staff often open a call, at a health center or any office.

**Jag har ont i halsen**: "I have a sore throat", literally I have pain in the throat. *Ont i* goes with any part of the body, with *the*: *ont i huvudet* (a headache), *ont i magen* (a stomachache). **Det gör ont** is "it hurts".

**Trettioåtta och fem**: 38.5 degrees. Sweden measures temperature in Celsius and writes decimals with a comma: *38,5*.

**I morse, i eftermiddag**: *i morse* is this morning, already past; *i morgon* is tomorrow, so don't mix them up. *I eftermiddag* is this afternoon.

**Passar det?**: "Does that suit you?" The answer is *Ja, det passar bra* or *Nej, det passar tyvärr inte*.

**Om du blir sämre …, kan du ringa**: when an *om*-clause (if …) comes first, it takes the first place, and the verb *kan* comes second, before *du*. The comma after the *om*-clause is optional. *Är det akut, ring 112* says the same with no *om*: the verb first.

**1177 and 112**: 1177 is Sweden's national number and website (1177.se) for health care advice; on the phone, nurses answer at any hour. 112 is the number for emergencies."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-health-0a> ,
                         <#q-health-0b> ,
                         <#q-health-0c> .

