<#ch-health-3>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-health> ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Another way it goes: the pharmacy

*The next day, Sam goes to the pharmacy near the station.*

**Sam:** Hej, jag behöver något mot ont i halsen.\\
*Hi, I need something for a sore throat.*

**Apotekaren:** De här sugtabletterna brukar hjälpa. Och drick mycket.\\
*These lozenges usually help. And drink plenty.*

**Sam:** Tack, jag tar dem.\\
*Thanks, I'll take them.*

**Apotekaren:** Krya på dig!\\
*Get well soon!*

*On the stairs at home, Sam meets Elin.*

**Elin:** Sam! Du ser sjuk ut. Har du sjukanmält dig?\\
*Sam! You look sick. Have you called in sick?*

**Sam:** Ja, jag har ringt Karin. Jag stannar hemma resten av veckan.\\
*Yes, I've called Karin. I'm staying home the rest of the week.*

**Elin:** Säg till om du behöver något. Jag kan handla åt dig.\\
*Let me know if you need anything. I can do your shopping for you.*

---

**Apotek**: a pharmacy. Medicine on prescription is sold only there; some medicines for pain and fever can also be bought in grocery stores.

**Brukar hjälpa**: "usually help". *Bruka* + a verb says what usually happens: *Jag brukar fika klockan tre*.

**Krya på dig!**: "Get well soon!", said to anyone who is ill, in person, in a text or on a card.

**Du ser sjuk ut**: "You look sick." *Se … ut* is to look a certain way, with *ut* at the end.

**Sjukanmäla sig**: to call in sick, a reflexive verb like *känna sig*. In Sweden you tell your employer on the first day you are ill, and from the eighth day a doctor's certificate (*läkarintyg*) is usually needed.

**Säg till om …**: "Let me know if …". **Handla åt dig**: *åt* is for, on someone's behalf.

**Culture: home when you are ill**: staying home with a fever, rather than going to work, is expected in Sweden. A parent who stays home with a sick child is said to *vabba*, from *VAB, vård av barn* (care of a child)."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-health-2a> ,
                         <#q-health-2b> ,
                         <#q-health-2c> .

<#q-health-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-11-18T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "Krya på dig!"@sv ;
    solid-memo:back "Get well soon!"@en ,
                    "Gute Besserung!"@de ,
                    "¡Que se mejore!"@es ,
                    "Bon rétablissement !"@fr ,
                    "빨리 나으세요!"@ko ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Said to anyone who is ill, by a pharmacist, a friend or a colleague, in person, in a text or on a card. Krya på sig is to get better."@en ,
                        "Sagt man zu jedem, der krank ist, ob in der Apotheke, unter Freunden oder Kollegen, persönlich, in einer Nachricht oder auf einer Karte. Krya på sig heißt gesund werden."@de ,
                        "Se le dice a cualquier persona enferma, sea un farmacéutico, un amigo o un compañero quien lo diga, en persona, en un mensaje o en una tarjeta. Krya på sig es mejorarse."@es ,
                        "Se dit à toute personne malade, par un pharmacien, un ami ou un collègue, de vive voix, par message ou sur une carte. Krya på sig veut dire se remettre, guérir."@fr ,
                        "약사, 친구, 동료가 아픈 사람에게 얼굴을 보고, 문자로, 또는 카드에 쓰는 말이에요. Krya på sig는 '회복하다'예요."@ko ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-health-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-health-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-health-2a-d3> .

